    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-heading">
      <h2 id="overview-heading">What the German Year is</h2>
      <p>
        To graduate from GJU you must spend one full academic year studying and training in
        Germany. The year splits in two, and the order is fixed: <strong>study semester
        first, internship semester second</strong>, unless you hold a scholarship other than
        the GJU-DAAD German Year scholarship.
      </p>

      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Semester 1 — study</h3>
          <p>
            Courses at one of GJU’s German partner universities, normally
            <strong>18–21 ECTS or 12 SWS</strong>. GJU recognises up to
            <strong>21 credit hours</strong>. Everything you take must appear in a signed
            Learning Agreement before you leave; a course that is not on it is not
            recognised, and German marks do not enter your GJU average — they are
            pass/fail.
          </p>
          <p class="card-note">
            <span lang="de">B.A. German and English for Business and Communication</span>
            and B.A. Translation students take courses equivalent to 15 credit hours. At
            most two courses may be taught in English, totalling no more than 12 ECTS.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>Semester 2 — internship</h3>
          <p>
            At least <strong>20 weeks full time</strong> with a company, recognised as
            <strong>12 credit hours</strong>. Up to six months counts, as long as your
            residence permit covers it. You cannot register any GJU course while you are on
            internship — the only exception is sitting a final exam for a course you already
            took and failed during the study semester.
          </p>
          <p class="card-note">
            You are responsible for finding the placement. The International Office, the
            Office for Industrial Links and the Project Office in Magdeburg support you;
            none of them place you.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="callout callout-warn">
        <strong>One full academic year, no shortcuts.</strong> You must spend
        1<sup>st</sup> + 2<sup>nd</sup> + summer semester in Germany, or 2<sup>nd</sup> +
        summer + 1<sup>st</sup>. Anyone who does not stay the full year may not register
        courses at GJU or start an internship in Jordan while they are supposed to be in
        Germany.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="before" aria-labelledby="before-heading">
      <h2 id="before-heading">Before you travel</h2>
      <p>
        Failing any prerequisite lets your School and the International Office stop you from
        travelling — <em>even at the last minute</em>, and you carry the financial loss. Check
        these early.
      </p>

      <h3>Academic prerequisites</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Credit hours you must have passed before leaving. German language hours do not count towards these totals.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Programme</th><th scope="col">Credit hours</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>All Engineering majors, including Architecture</td><td>90 CH</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Management &amp; Logistic Sciences, Computer Science, Design &amp; Visual Communication</td><td>85 CH</td></tr>
            <tr><td>B.A. German and English for Business and Communication; B.A. Translation (study plan from 1<sup>st</sup> semester 2014/15)</td><td>72 CH</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>GPA ≥ 61 %</strong> when you apply — unless you can prove fewer than 33 credit hours remain to graduate, the 24 German Year hours included. <strong>GPA ≥ 60 %</strong> by the time you travel.</li>
        <li>All obligatory courses named by your School or Department, passed.</li>
        <li><strong>Five levels of German</strong> from the German Language Center (School of Applied Humanities and Languages students: five levels from their own School).</li>
        <li><strong>Level 5 English</strong>, and every <strong>099-level course</strong>.</li>
        <li>The <strong>160-hour Field Training in Jordan</strong> (B.A. Translation and B.A. German and English for Business and Communication students who started before 1<sup>st</sup> semester 2017/18 are exempt).</li>
        <li>Application documents — CV, cover letter, certificates — prepared <em>before</em> you fly, and the OIL internship training seminar attended.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="callout">
        From 1<sup>st</sup> semester 2021/22 the old exemption for outside language
        certificates is gone. Sit the German placement test, or present a certificate no
        older than twelve months from Goethe-Institut, ÖSD, telc, TestDaF, DSD or the
        Kultusministerkonferenz — it can place you in a higher level, but never out of
        German VI.
      </p>

      <h3>Money, visa and insurance</h3>
      <div class="card-grid three">
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Blocked account</h4>
          <p><strong>10,332 €</strong> plus <strong>189 €</strong> Fintiba fees, deposited via Fintiba with Sutor Bank — roughly 10,500 € for twelve months. No proof of transfer, no visa process.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Health insurance</h4>
          <p>Travel health insurance bought in Jordan covering your <strong>first three months</strong>, plus German public insurance — the Embassy accepts only <strong>DAK</strong>, obtained through Fintiba, at about <strong>110 €/month</strong>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Liability insurance</h4>
          <p><span lang="de">Haftpflichtversicherung</span> is not mandatory but strongly advised from the day you arrive. About <strong>60 €/year</strong> through Fintiba.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Visa deadlines</h4>
          <p><strong>10 June</strong> for a 1<sup>st</sup>-semester start, <strong>10 December</strong> for a 2<sup>nd</sup>-semester start. The visa itself takes <strong>6–8 weeks</strong> after the appointment.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Housing</h4>
          <p>Dorm rooms run <strong>200–500 €/month</strong>, usually with a two-month deposit — some halls want all six months up front. Take any offer you get; the private market is tight and unfurnished.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Living costs</h4>
          <p><strong>700–1,000 €/month</strong> depending on the city. Several host universities also charge registration and service fees of <strong>250–700 €</strong> that GJU cannot influence.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="callout callout-warn">
        <strong>Do not contact the host universities yourself.</strong> The International
        Office runs the application, the distribution and the deadlines. Distribution
        follows five published criteria — partner requirements, a balanced spread of
        strong and weak students, social and gender considerations, geographic balance and
        GJU’s strategic goals — so a higher GPA gives you no priority over a classmate.
      </p>
    </section>

