    <section id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-heading">
      <h2 id="timeline-heading">The year, step by step</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Nomination and distribution</h3>
          <p>
            Your School sends the application link in the first or second week of the
            semester. You name five partner universities, in no particular order; the
            International Office and your Exchange Coordinator decide together. Not everyone
            gets one of their five.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Applications, Fintiba, visa</h3>
          <p>
            University forms, Learning Agreement, transcripts, language certificates, three
            biometric photos and three signed passport copies. Then the blocked account, then
            the Embassy appointment and fingerprints. Apply early — it gives the IO room to
            find you another seat if a partner rejects you.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Study semester</h3>
          <p>
            Courses at the host university under a signed Learning Agreement. Changes need
            approval from the host university and your Exchange Coordinator, cc’d to the IO
            and your Head of Department, at the start of the semester.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Search and apply — from week one</h3>
          <p>
            Within two weeks of arriving, take your documents to the Career Center, the
            International Office or your German contact professor and have them checked.
            Then start applying. Expect to send fifty or more applications.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Interview, offer, contract</h3>
          <p>
            The moment you are accepted, send the company details and the contract to your
            Exchange Coordinator (cc the Office for Industrial Links) for approval, and tell
            the Project Office in Magdeburg. Then politely cancel your other interviews.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Twenty weeks of work</h3>
          <p>
            Stay enrolled at your host university for the second semester — your visa and
            work permit depend on it. Keep an internship diary from day one; the report is
            far easier to write while the work is still fresh.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Certificate, report, recognition</h3>
          <p>
            Collect a signed and stamped <span lang="de">Praktikumszeugnis</span>, get the
            report signed and stamped, and hand both to the Office for Industrial Links
            within two months of the next semester.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

