    <section id="cv" aria-labelledby="cv-heading">
      <h2 id="cv-heading">The CV (<span lang="de">Lebenslauf</span>)</h2>
      <p>
        A German CV is a table, not a story, and it is the first impression the company gets.
        Rewrite it for each application: decide every time which facts actually matter for
        that job.
      </p>

      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Form</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Word document, table form, headed <span lang="de">Lebenslauf</span>.</li>
            <li>Chronological or reverse chronological — pick one and hold it.</li>
            <li>One page. Two at the very most.</li>
            <li>Calibri, Arial, Tahoma or Verdana; never below 11 pt.</li>
            <li>Dates always <code>MM/YYYY</code>, month by month, with no gaps.</li>
            <li>Bold for emphasis, sparingly — not bold <em>and</em> italic.</li>
            <li>Headlines left-aligned.</li>
            <li>Application photo, top right. Have it taken properly.</li>
            <li>City, date and a scanned signature at the bottom.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Sections</h3>
          <dl class="facts compact">
            <dt lang="de">Persönliche Daten</dt>
            <dd>Name as in your passport (first and last only), German address, phone with area code, a serious e-mail address, date and place of birth, nationality. Marital status is optional.</dd>
            <dt lang="de">Schulbildung</dt>
            <dd>Your Tawjihi with the final grade.</dd>
            <dt lang="de">Studium</dt>
            <dd>Programme, university and city, and the degree you are working towards.</dd>
            <dt lang="de">Praxiserfahrung</dt>
            <dd>Not just company names — the tasks you did. That is what convinces.</dd>
            <dt lang="de">Weiterbildungen</dt>
            <dd>Only recent training that is relevant and ended in a certificate.</dd>
            <dt lang="de">Zusatzqualifikationen</dt>
            <dd>Languages and software, each with an honest level.</dd>
            <dt lang="de">Interessen</dt>
            <dd>Team hobbies read well. Skip reading and swimming; think twice about anything risky or hard to explain.</dd>
          </dl>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="callout">
        <strong>Careful with <span lang="de">Nationalität</span>.</strong> Under
        <span lang="de">Nationalität</span> you write the nationality —
        <span lang="de">jordanisch</span>. Under
        <span lang="de">Staatsangehörigkeit</span> you write the country —
        <span lang="de">Jordanien</span>.
      </p>

      <details class="check">
        <summary>CV checklist</summary>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Spelling and structure checked end to end; clear and readable throughout.</li>
          <li>One or two pages, no more.</li>
          <li>Contact details complete: name, address, phone, mobile, e-mail.</li>
          <li><span lang="de">Persönliche Daten</span> complete.</li>
          <li>Logical headings and sub-items, in a logical order.</li>
          <li>Chronological or reverse chronological throughout.</li>
          <li>Every date in <code>MM/YYYY</code>, month by month.</li>
          <li>Practical experience names the company and the city.</li>
          <li>Nothing unnecessary left in.</li>
          <li>Languages listed with an assessment of each.</li>
          <li>Computer skills listed with an assessment.</li>
          <li>Every term explained; no abbreviations, no stray English.</li>
          <li>City, current date and signature at the foot.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <p class="doc-links">
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/application-process/praktikumsinfo1_lebenslauf_en_neues_logo.pdf') | url }}">
