    <section id="cover-letter" aria-labelledby="cover-letter-heading">
      <h2 id="cover-letter-heading">The cover letter (<span lang="de">Anschreiben</span>)</h2>
      <p>
        This is where you argue that <em>you</em> are the right person. The reader will spend
        very little time on it, so it has to be short and interesting. One page — roughly
        four to eight sentences of body text.
      </p>

      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Form</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Left-aligned, not justified — it looks livelier.</li>
            <li>One font throughout, 11–12 pt, single line spacing.</li>
            <li>Real paragraphs; hyphenate by hand, not by autocorrect.</li>
            <li>No abbreviations anywhere. Do not start sentence after sentence with <span lang="de">„ich“</span>.</li>
            <li>Same page header as your CV, so the two look like one document.</li>
            <li>Sign it by hand — scan the signature for e-mail applications.</li>
            <li>Note <span lang="de">„Anlagen“</span> under your signature; you need not list them.</li>
            <li>IT students especially: follow <a href="https://www.din5008.de" rel="external nofollow">DIN 5008</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>The text must answer</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Why this internship? What is genuinely interesting about it?</li>
            <li>Which of your experiences are relevant to it?</li>
            <li>Which characteristics describe you — and what evidence backs each one?</li>
            <li>What are your goals?</li>
            <li>How do you meet the requirements in the advertisement?</li>
            <li>Why <em>you</em>, and not someone else?</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="card-note">
            Close by summarising what speaks for you, then ask politely for an interview.
            Sign off <span lang="de">„Mit freundlichen Grüßen“</span>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3>The address block and subject line</h3>
      <p>
        Address a person, not a department. The name is in the advertisement or on the
        company site; if it is nowhere, phone and ask. The subject line carries the exact
        job title, the reference number, where you saw the advertisement and when it was
        published — in bold.
      </p>
      <figure class="sample">
        <pre lang="de">Stadtwerke München
Frau Christa Rösner
Personalpolitik/Personalmarketing
Emmy-Noether-Straße 2
80287 München

<strong>Bewerbung um ein Praktikum im Bereich Softwareentwicklung (Java) ab März 2011</strong>
hier: Ihre Anzeige in der Süddeutschen Zeitung vom 13.01.2011

Sehr geehrte Frau Rösner,</pre>
        <figcaption>
          Never <span lang="de">„Sehr geehrte Damen und Herren“</span> when you can name the
          person. Never <span lang="de">„Hiermit bewerbe ich mich…“</span> when you can open
          with something about them: <span lang="de">„Sie suchen einen Praktikanten…“</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="callout callout-warn">
        <strong>The copy-and-paste trap.</strong> You will reuse text across dozens of
        applications; that is fine. But read every letter again before it goes, and check
        above all that the company name and the contact person are the right ones.
      </p>

      <details class="check">
        <summary>Cover letter checklist</summary>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Standard page format, real paragraphs, same look as the CV.</li>
          <li>One page; single line spacing; one font at 11–12 pt.</li>
          <li>No abbreviations, no chain of sentences starting with <span lang="de">„ich“</span>.</li>
          <li>Whole document re-checked for grammar, spelling and formatting.</li>
          <li>Page header with all contact details, well laid out.</li>
          <li>Company name and address copied correctly, with the responsible person added.</li>
          <li>Bold subject line carrying every piece of available information.</li>
          <li>Personal salutation, individual opening, no stock phrases.</li>
          <li>Earlier phone call or e-mail referred to, if there was one.</li>
          <li>You explain your interest in this company and this internship.</li>
          <li>Previous jobs and internships summarised — not the CV repeated.</li>
          <li>Your skills tied back to the requirements in the advertisement, each with a reason.</li>
          <li>You explain why you in particular fit the position.</li>
          <li>A polite request for an interview at the end.</li>
          <li><span lang="de">„Mit freundlichen Grüßen“</span>, the current date, and a scanned signature.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <p class="doc-links">
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/application-process/praktikumsinfo2_anschreiben_en_neues_logo.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Internship Information Part 2 — The cover letter
        </a>
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/samples/cover-letter/muster_anschreiben1.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Sample letter 1
        </a>
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/samples/cover-letter/muster_anschreiben2.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Sample letter 2
        </a>
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/samples/cover-letter/kreativesanschreiben.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Creative sample letter
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="sending" aria-labelledby="sending-heading">
      <h2 id="sending-heading">Sending the application</h2>

      <h3>What goes in the PDF</h3>
      <p>
        An e-mail application is one PDF named
        <code lang="de">Bewerbung Vorname Nachname</code>, in this order:
      </p>
      <ol class="numbered">
        <li>Cover letter (<span lang="de">Anschreiben</span>)</li>
        <li>CV (<span lang="de">Lebenslauf</span>) — these two may be merged into one file</li>
        <li>Certificates (<span lang="de">Zeugnisse und Referenzen</span>) — <strong>all scanned into a single readable PDF</strong></li>
        <li>Architecture and Design students only: a portfolio as a separate PDF, <strong>under 3 MB</strong></li>
      </ol>

      <p>The certificates section contains:</p>
      <ul class="checklist tight">
        <li>Confirmation of enrolment from your German university <em>and</em> from GJU</li>
        <li><span lang="de">Bestätigung Pflichtpraktikum</span> from the International Office</li>
        <li>Your current GJU transcript</li>
        <li>Your Tawjihi certificate or equivalent</li>
        <li>Letters of reference from earlier internships, training or voluntary work</li>
        <li>A letter of recommendation from a GJU professor — optional, and worth asking for</li>
        <li>The GJU internship guidelines (<span lang="de">GJU-Praktikumsrichtlinien</span>)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Three ways to apply</h3>
      <div class="card-grid three">
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>By e-mail</h4>
          <p>
            Attach the PDF and keep the message itself short. Send it to yourself first —
            pasted text breaks line endings, and umlauts and HTML formatting do not survive
            every mail client. Plain text only.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Online form</h4>
          <p>
            Many companies require it. If they do, use it — do not send your documents to
            some other address instead. Take your time and fill in every field carefully.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <h4>Speculative (<span lang="de">Initiativbewerbung</span>)</h4>
          <p>
            When there is no advertisement. Phone first to ask whether they take interns and
            who should receive it. Same form and structure as a normal application; say in
            the subject that it is speculative and which position you want.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <figure class="sample">
        <pre lang="de">Betreff: Bewerbung für ein Praktikum (Bezeichnung und/oder Stellennummer)

Sehr geehrte Frau …,

im Anhang finden Sie meine Bewerbungsunterlagen für ein Praktikum in Ihrem
Unternehmen im Bereich … . Ich würde mich freuen, ein Praktikum bei Ihnen
absolvieren zu können. Für Rückfragen stehe ich Ihnen selbstverständlich per
Mail oder unter der Telefonnummer […] zur Verfügung.

Mit freundlichen Grüßen
Vorname Nachname</pre>
        <figcaption>A body for the e-mail itself. The application lives in the attachment, not here.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="doc-links">
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/application-process/praktikumsinfo3_bewerbungsverfahren_und_praktikum_en_august_2019_1_0.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Internship Information Part 3 — The application procedure
        </a>
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/search-tools/jobborsen.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> <span lang="de">Jobbörsen</span> — job board list
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="interview" aria-labelledby="interview-heading">
      <h2 id="interview-heading">The interview</h2>
      <p>
        An invitation already means they liked the application. The conversation usually
        runs an hour at most and follows a predictable shape.
      </p>

      <h3>Before you go</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Research them properly.</strong> Employee count, divisions, products, current projects. Being able to say “for your project XY I could contribute…” is worth more than any stock answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Match the tone.</strong> A young internet company and a traditional bank are different conversations.</li>
        <li><strong>Dress for the branch.</strong> Conservative in a traditional company, relaxed in a creative one — clean, neat, and something you are comfortable in. Keep jewellery, make-up and perfume restrained.</li>
        <li><strong>Plan the journey.</strong> Aim to arrive about 15 minutes early. Late makes a bad impression; two hours early is not much better.</li>
        <li><strong>Bring the paperwork:</strong> a copy of your application, the invitation, and copies of your certificates and references.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>In the room</h3>
      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h4>Do</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Knock so they can hear you, and wait to be asked in.</li>
            <li>Introduce yourself with first and last name; return an offered handshake firmly.</li>
            <li>Sit only when invited, feet flat on the floor, well back on the chair.</li>
            <li>Catch the interviewer’s name and use it. Ask again straight away if you missed it.</li>
            <li>Talk clearly more than half the time — they want to hear about you.</li>
            <li>Hold eye contact without staring. Ask if you may take notes.</li>
            <li>Ask when something is unclear; it shows interest.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h4>Don’t</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Accept a cigarette — politely refuse; smoking in interviews is not done in Germany. A drink is fine.</li>
            <li>Fold your arms or build a wall of cups and notepads.</li>
            <li>Cross your legs, sit on the edge of the chair, or tilt it.</li>
            <li>Fidget with your hands.</li>
            <li>Rush. Excitement makes people speak too fast.</li>
            <li>Use filler like <span lang="de">„sag ich mal“</span> or <span lang="de">„man“</span>.</li>
            <li>Crowd the interviewer — keep a comfortable distance.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3>The five phases</h3>
      <ol class="steps compact" lang="de">
        <li><h4>Begrüßung und Einleitung</h4><p>Haben Sie gut hierher gefunden? Wie war Ihre Anreise?</p></li>
        <li><h4>Kennenlernen des Bewerbers und seiner Motivation</h4><p>Erzählen Sie uns bitte etwas über sich und Ihren bisherigen Werdegang. Warum haben Sie sich für dieses Studium entschieden? Warum an der GJU? Warum sind Sie zum Studieren nach Deutschland gekommen? Was ist Ihr berufliches Ziel? Was sind Ihre Stärken und Schwächen? Was machen Sie in Ihrer Freizeit?</p></li>
        <li><h4>Gespräch über das Unternehmen und Praktikumsinhalte</h4><p>Was wissen Sie über unser Unternehmen und unsere Produkte? Warum haben Sie sich gerade bei uns beworben? Was erwarten Sie von Ihrem Praktikum? Welche Bereiche interessieren Sie? Welche Fragen haben Sie an uns?</p></li>
        <li><h4>Vertragsverhandlungen</h4><p>Wann soll das Praktikum anfangen? Wie lange soll es dauern? Was für eine Praktikantenvergütung stellen Sie sich vor?</p></li>
        <li><h4>Abschluss</h4><p>Wie geht es weiter? Bis wann ist eine Entscheidung zu erwarten?</p></li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Questions worth preparing</h3>
      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h4>What they will ask</h4>
          <dl class="facts compact">
            <dt>Why us?</dt>
            <dd>Name what is actually good about the company and the field you want to learn.</dd>
            <dt>What do you expect from the internship?</dt>
            <dd>What you want to learn; which areas you want to see.</dd>
            <dt>Strengths and weaknesses?</dt>
            <dd>Everyone has both. Pair a weakness with a matching strength, and avoid the worn-out “I expect too much of myself”. Saying your German is not perfect but improving is honest and lands well.</dd>
            <dt>Where in 2, 5, 10 years?</dt>
            <dd>Be ambitious and concrete — a master’s in Germany, a direction you are heading. Tie it back to them where you can.</dd>
            <dt>What do you know about us?</dt>
            <dd>Read the site and the press beforehand. Banks, authorities and media may also ask about politics and economics: show you follow the news, and offer your own opinion only carefully.</dd>
          </dl>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h4>What you should ask</h4>
          <p class="card-note">Keep one or two for the end. Ask open questions — who, what, how. Avoid “why”: it can sound like criticism.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>How much of the work is individual and how much is team work?</li>
            <li>What happens during the introductory phase?</li>
            <li>How are interns supervised here?</li>
            <li>Which departments will I see? Which tasks will I be given?</li>
            <li>Who are my supervisors and colleagues?</li>
            <li>What does my workplace look like — could I see it?</li>
            <li>You mentioned project XY on your site; can you tell me more?</li>
            <li>Could you help me find a flat in …?</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="card-note">Pay and working-time rules belong before you sign the contract — not necessarily in the first conversation.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="callout">
        <strong>You do not have to answer everything.</strong> Questions about your finances,
        marriage, wish for children, pregnancy, illnesses or religion are generally not
        allowed, and anything unrelated to the job need not be answered. The exceptions:
        an illness relevant to the work, a contagious disease, or a condition likely to make
        you unfit for work must be disclosed on request. More broadly, you must tell an
        employer anything you can recognise as material to the contract.
      </p>

      <h3>On the phone</h3>
      <p>
        Phone screening is part of many selection procedures, and you may call them first —
        any initiative is worth it. Say your name clearly, ask three smart questions in a
        fresh voice, be short and precise, and get off the line. A first call also gives you
        a far better opening than the usual one:
        <span lang="de">„Herzlichen Dank für das nette Gespräch vom vergangenen Freitag, bei
        dem Sie mich ermutigt haben, meine Unterlagen einzureichen.“</span> Keep your CV,
        certificates and the advertisement in front of you — they will scan you back.
      </p>

      <p class="doc-links">
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/application-process/praktikumsinfo4_bewerbungsgespraech_en_neues_logo.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Internship Information Part 4 — The interview
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="during" aria-labelledby="during-heading">
      <h2 id="during-heading">During the internship</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Get the contract.</strong> The company sends an internship contract for you to sign. Check that the placement is not shorter than 20 weeks.</li>
        <li><strong>Stay enrolled</strong> at your German host university for the second semester. Your visa and work permit depend on it.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep a diary.</strong> Note your tasks daily or weekly. The report is built from these notes; writing it from memory afterwards is far harder.</li>
        <li><strong>Treat it as a real job.</strong> Office hours, break times, holiday rules — the contract applies to you like anyone else.</li>
        <li><strong>No courses.</strong> You may not register GJU courses during the internship semester.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask for the certificate.</strong> Tell the company early that you need a signed and stamped <span lang="de">Praktikumszeugnis</span> at the end.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="callout callout-warn">
        <strong>If something goes wrong</strong> — you feel underused, overwhelmed, or you
        cannot get on with a colleague — start with a calm conversation with the person
        involved or your supervisor. If that does not help, tell the Project Office in
        Magdeburg, your Exchange Officer at GJU, or the Office for Industrial Links.
        <strong>Never quit an internship before you have another agreement in place.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Take the placement seriously and it can turn into a bachelor thesis or a job. Show
        interest in what you work on, bring your own ideas, offer help, and follow the
        company’s rules.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="after" aria-labelledby="after-heading">
      <h2 id="after-heading">Certificate and report</h2>
      <p class="callout callout-warn">
        Both documents are due <strong>within two months of the next semester</strong>.
        Hand them in late and you are blocked from registering.
      </p>

      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Internship certificate</h3>
          <p>
            An official signed and stamped <span lang="de">Praktikumszeugnis</span> from the
            company, in German or English, carrying:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The company logo and name, and the supervisor’s name</li>
            <li>Your personal details and the internship dates</li>
            <li>A description of your tasks</li>
            <li>An evaluation of your performance</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="card-note">
            Deliver the original plus <strong>three hard copies</strong> to the Office for
            Industrial Links; the original comes back to you. The company may also post it
            to: German Jordanian University, Office for Industrial Links, P.O. Box 35247,
            Amman 11180, Jordan.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>Internship report</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>15–20 pages</strong> (5,000–7,000 words)</li>
            <li>Written in <strong>English</strong>, with a <strong>1–2 page German summary</strong> at the end</li>
            <li>Page numbers on every page</li>
            <li><strong>Clip it — do not bind or file it</strong></li>
            <li>Signed and stamped by the company</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="card-note">
            Some companies have their own outline or a secrecy agreement — ask your
            supervisor. If you cannot finish before you leave, e-mail a scan to the company
            to be signed, stamped and returned. Your Exchange Coordinator approves it; if it
            is not up to standard, you rewrite it.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3>Report structure</h3>
      <ol class="numbered spaced">
        <li>
          <strong>Cover page</strong> — your name and GJU student ID, the title
          (“Internship at Company XY”), start and finish dates, number of weeks, your
          company supervisor and your GJU advisor, with space left for both to sign and stamp.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Privacy statement</strong>, if the company requires one.</li>
        <li><strong>Table of contents</strong> with page numbers, plus lists of tables and figures and the references.</li>
        <li><strong>Introduction</strong> — why this company, and what you did in outline.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>The company</strong> — brief history, departments, full address and web
          links; its sector, products and services, and who its customers are; whether it
          operates worldwide and where; an organisation chart and the employee count.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Internship activities</strong> — the main part. A typical working day and
          the unusual ones; difficulties with the work, the language and the culture; your
          supervisor and team; your tasks and what you learned; the tools you used; a
          special project and its result; any research you did; and a comparison of what you
          learned at university with what you saw in practice. Include real work samples —
          graphs, pictures, data, drawings, calculations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Evaluation</strong> — skills gained, whether it met your expectations, the
          responsibilities you were trusted with, how it shapes your career plans, how it
          connects to your studies, the main challenges, your relationship with supervisor
          and colleagues, and whether you would recommend the company to other GJU students.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Conclusion</strong> — the key takeaways.</li>
        <li><strong>Appendices</strong> — charts, graphs, pictures, code.</li>
        <li><strong>References.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Summary in German</strong> — one to two pages.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="muted">
        Your School or study programme may add its own requirements. Ask your academic
        advisor at GJU.
      </p>

      <p class="doc-links">
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/after-the-internship/internship_report_instructions.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Internship report instructions
        </a>
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/after-the-internship/required_internship_documents_2015_46.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Required internship documents (2015/46)
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="rules" aria-labelledby="rules-heading">
      <h2 id="rules-heading">Rules and edge cases</h2>

      <h3>Where the internship may be</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">Who has to approve it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>German or international company <strong>in Germany</strong></td><td class="ok">Preferred — has priority</td></tr>
            <tr><td>German or international company in Austria, Switzerland or Luxembourg</td><td class="ok">Approved easily</td></tr>
            <tr><td>German company in a non-German-speaking European country</td><td>Your Dean, explicitly</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Non-German company in a non-German-speaking European country</td><td>The President, explicitly</td></tr>
            <tr><td>German company outside Europe</td><td>Deans’ Council recommendation <em>and</em> the President</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Anything else</td><td class="no">Generally not approved</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The placement must be with an industry, company or institution. Other placements — a
        university laboratory, for instance — are possible only with a genuine link to your
        study programme and the explicit approval of your Exchange Coordinator and Dean.
      </p>

      <h3>If you do not complete the 20 weeks</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>What happens if you return to Jordan with an incomplete internship.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Completed in Germany</th>
              <th scope="col">Weeks required elsewhere</th>
              <th scope="col">Credit hours to register and pay</th>
              <th scope="col">On your transcript</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>None, or under 5 weeks</td><td>30 weeks</td><td>12 CH</td><td>Fail 12 · Pass 0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5 to under 10 weeks</td><td>20 weeks</td><td>9 CH</td><td>Fail 9 · Pass 3</td></tr>
            <tr><td>10 to under 15 weeks</td><td>12 weeks</td><td>6 CH</td><td>Fail 6 · Pass 6</td></tr>
            <tr><td>15 to under 20 weeks</td><td>6 weeks</td><td>3 CH</td><td>Fail 3 · Pass 9</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Going back to Germany or another German-speaking country to finish counts as a
        continuation of the same internship instead. Any internship in Jordan or elsewhere
        needs the approval of your Dean and the head of the Office for Industrial Links, and
        the place and duration appear on your transcript. Once approved, you may not split
        or interrupt it to register courses.
      </p>

      <h3>Internships outside Germany (Corona regulations)</h3>
      <p>
        Exceptional rules apply to the outgoing groups of 2019/20, 2020/21 and — with
        limitations — 1<sup>st</sup> semester 2021/22. A substitute internship in Jordan or
        elsewhere needs prior approval from the OIL Director and your Dean; the company need
        not be German; only the 20 required weeks must be completed; and the certificate
        will state that the internship was not conducted in Germany.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Credit hours to register at GJU for a substitute internship, and the documents required for a split internship.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Weeks already done in Germany</th>
              <th scope="col">Weeks needed outside Germany</th>
              <th scope="col">Credit hours</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>0 to under 5</td><td>20–16</td><td>12 CH</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5 to under 10</td><td>15–11</td><td>9 CH</td></tr>
            <tr><td>10 to under 15</td><td>10–6</td><td>6 CH</td></tr>
            <tr><td>15 to under 20</td><td>5–1</td><td>3 CH</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist tight">
        <li><strong>Under 5 weeks</strong> — a signed certificate or company confirmation only, no report.</li>
        <li><strong>5–9 weeks</strong> — signed certificate plus a 2–3 page summary report, which the company need not sign.</li>
        <li><strong>10 weeks or more</strong> — certificate plus the full report, both signed and stamped.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted">
        Remote internships from home office are allowed where the company’s own precautions
        require it. Students still registered at a German university for the online
        internship do not register or pay tuition at GJU; students who are not, do. You may
        not register GJU courses in parallel with an internship, and you may not postpone
        the internship in order to take an extra study semester in Germany.
      </p>

      <h3>Graduation project in Germany</h3>
      <p>
        Many students are offered a bachelor graduation project or thesis in Germany, either
        through a company or a partner university. Two routes exist, and the company route
        requires that your <strong>20 weeks are already complete</strong>, that the project
        is <strong>clearly distinct</strong> from the internship work, and that it runs at
        least 12 weeks for 3 credit hours or 16 weeks for 6. The documentation is in English,
        signed by the company advisor and stamped, and the defence happens at GJU as normal.
        You are responsible for keeping legal residence in Germany throughout.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">
        You may not register graduation project courses before finishing the German Year
        requirements, unless your study plan allows it or you have completed at least half of
        the international internship. Additional study or internship semesters are possible
        only after the German Year is complete, and they fall outside it.
      </p>

      <p class="doc-links">
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/rules-and-regulations/german_year_manual_final_0.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> German Year Manual
        </a>
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/rules-and-regulations/gju_praktikumsrichtlinien_stand_september_2021.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> <span lang="de">GJU-Praktikumsrichtlinien</span>
        </a>
        <a class="doc-link" href="{{ (docs + '/rules-and-regulations/regulations_for_corona_related_internships_outside_germany_all_batches_20210221.pdf') | url }}">
          <span class="doc-kind">PDF</span> Corona-related internships outside Germany
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
    <section id="staying" aria-labelledby="staying-heading">
      <h2 id="staying-heading">Staying longer than planned</h2>
      <p>
        Internships often run past the end of the semester. Which route you take depends on
        how far past.
      </p>

      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>One to two months over — <span lang="de">Fiktionsbescheinigung</span></h3>
          <p>
            A provisional certificate that extends your visa. Go to the local
            <span lang="de">Ausländerbehörde</span> with:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Your contract with the company</li>
            <li>Your GJU enrolment certificate</li>
            <li>Proof of funds — a refilled blocked account or an internship salary of at least <strong>861 € a month</strong></li>
            <li>A letter from your insurer confirming your cover for the extended period</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="card-note">They may ask for more; bring everything you have.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>More than three months over — full enrolment</h3>
          <p>You will be enrolled for a whole extra semester. Three things at university level:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Written approval of the extension from your School — an e-mail from your Exchange Coordinator is enough. Forward it to your mobility coordinator.</li>
            <li>Confirmation from your German host university that it will enrol you for an additional semester. <strong>Your visa and work permit depend on this.</strong></li>
            <li>Postpone your GJU studies using the postponing-studies form from the GJU required-forms page.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="card-note">
            Then extend the visa, ideally no later than a month before it expires, proving
            continued enrolment, funds (refilled blocked account or salary of at least
            861 €/month) and insurance cover.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

